  "actual_producer_bindings": [
    {
      "id": "thm-indecomposable-projectives-correspond-to-indecomposable-soergel-modules",
      "path": "items/thm-indecomposable-projectives-correspond-to-indecomposable-soergel-modules.md",
      "sha256": "db4fedeb96ef57616567cff35efa193dec3dd37e8b02c753c4ec7e4e202ef18c",
      "actual_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and work in a regular integral type-A block over\n$\\mathbb C$. With the page's right wall-crossing convention and left\n$C$-module values of $V$, there is an equivalence\n$$V\\colon\\operatorname{Proj}(\\mathcal O_\\lambda)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\{\\text{ungraded Soergel modules }B\\otimes_S\\mathbb C\\},\\qquad P(w\\cdot\\lambda)\\longmapsto\\bar B_{w^{-1}}.$$\nThe target is the additive category of direct summands of the right reductions\nof polynomial Bott-Samelson bimodules, or equivalently of iterated\n$C\\otimes_{C^s}(-)$ applied to the trivial module $\\mathbb C$, not to the\nregular module $C$. For $w=s_1\\cdots s_k$, the projective is the unique new\ntop-label summand of $\\theta_{s_k}\\cdots\\theta_{s_1}\\Delta(\\lambda)$; applying\n$V$ gives the new summand of $\\overline{B_{s_k}\\cdots B_{s_1}}$, thus the\ninverse label. These normalized indecomposable polynomial lifts supply\nself-dual auxiliary gradings; in rank one the centered degrees are $-1,1$,\nwith degrees $0,2$ after an uncentered shift. Forgetting grading recovers the\nprojective dictionary. More generally the ungraded equivalence with\nWeyl-group Soergel modules holds for every complex semisimple regular integral\nblock; the type-A diagrammatic label comparison applies only in type A.",
      "review": "Actual Statement and consumed proof/context clauses read; inverse labels/pre-reduction ranks/character identity match the revised consumers. Character supplier F2/Proof2.1 normalization repaired separately with Statement unchanged."
    },
    {
      "id": "prop-verma-flag-multiplicities-are-standard-filtration-ranks-under-v",
      "path": "items/prop-verma-flag-multiplicities-are-standard-filtration-ranks-under-v.md",
      "sha256": "f5eb11eef3a7e375471d5ebf7fee37c28969c3db80cc7b5489365bda02a660cd",
      "actual_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For a projective $P$ in a regular integral type-A\nblock, choose its polynomial Soergel lift $\\mathsf B(P)$ determined by the\npreceding projective dictionary: if $P=\\bigoplus_wP(w\\cdot\\lambda)^{m_w}$, take\n$\\mathsf B(P)=\\bigoplus_wB_{w^{-1}}^{m_w}$, so that its right reduction is\n$V(P)$ after forgetting auxiliary grading. Write $r_{x,d}(\\mathsf B(P))$ for\nthe graded multiplicity of the polynomial standard graph module supported at\n$x$ in its standard support filtration, with the standard-generator\nnormalization of the polynomial Soergel category. Then\n$$(P:\\Delta(y\\cdot\\lambda))=\\sum_d r_{y^{-1},d}(\\mathsf B(P)).$$\nEquivalently the polynomial Soergel character of this lift, evaluated at $v=1$\nand with inverse labels, recovers the ordinary Verma-flag character of $P$.\nThese are ranks before reduction; no separated Bruhat-support filtration\nintrinsic to the finite-dimensional module $V(P)$ is asserted, since the graph\nsupports coalesce at the origin. The same ungraded multiplicity comparison\nholds with the corresponding Weyl-group polynomial/deformed lifts in other\ncomplex semisimple types.",
      "review": "Actual Statement and consumed proof/context clauses read; inverse labels/pre-reduction ranks/character identity match the revised consumers. Character supplier F2/Proof2.1 normalization repaired separately with Statement unchanged."
    },
    {
      "id": "thm-soergels-character-conjecture",
      "path": "items/thm-soergels-character-conjecture.md",
      "sha256": "c7acc4bf0c63684f12649e5acc12096b6dbd249dd45783493eb5b36a2b436269",
      "actual_statement": "For every $w\\in S_n$ the character of the indecomposable type-$A$ Soergel bimodule equals the Kazhdan-Lusztig basis element: $\\operatorname{ch}(B_w^R)=\\underline H_w$. Equivalently, the class $[B_w^R]$ in the split Grothendieck group corresponds to $\\underline H_w$ under the Hecke algebra isomorphism, and the extension of scalars of the rational $B_w$ categorifies the Kazhdan-Lusztig basis. Moreover the hard Lefschetz property $\\mathrm hL(w)$ and the Hodge-Riemann relations $\\mathrm{HR}(w)$ hold for every $w$, with the standard signs, and the same identities hold for the rational category by the base-change comparison. Finally, for every right descent $xs<x$ the matrix of $L_\\zeta$ on $B_xB_s=B_x(1)\\oplus B_x(-1)$ is $\\begin{pmatrix}\\rho\\cdot(-)&0\\\\ \\zeta\\rho(\\alpha_s^\\vee)&\\rho\\cdot(-)\\end{pmatrix}$; for $\\zeta>0$, $\\mathrm hL(x)$ implies $\\mathrm hL(x,s)_\\zeta$ without any additional character hypothesis, since $\\mathrm S$ is now established for all elements, while at $\\zeta=0$ centered hard Lefschetz fails.",
      "review": "Actual Statement and consumed proof/context clauses read; inverse labels/pre-reduction ranks/character identity match the revised consumers. Character supplier F2/Proof2.1 normalization repaired separately with Statement unchanged."
    }
  ],
